  <header>
    <div class="header-left">
      <h1>Plant-Based & Vegan Diets</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Built around whole plant foods, with different rules for how strict to go.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="ghost-btn" href="/diets/">← Back to Diets</a>
  </header>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">What Are Plant-Based and Vegan Diets?</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Plant-based and vegan are related but distinct ways of eating built around minimizing or excluding
        animal products. A plant-based diet centers meals on whole plant foods — fruits, vegetables, legumes,
        whole grains, nuts, and seeds — without necessarily excluding meat, dairy, or eggs entirely. A vegan
        diet is the strict version: it fully excludes all animal-derived foods and ingredients, including meat,
        poultry, seafood, dairy, eggs, and honey. In short, vegan is a strict subset of plant-based, not simply
        another name for it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How It Works</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        "Plant-based" and "vegan" get used interchangeably, but they're not the same thing. <strong>Vegan</strong> is the strict version: it excludes all animal-derived foods and ingredients —
        meat, poultry, seafood, dairy, eggs, honey, and often gelatin or other animal-derived additives. <strong>Plant-based</strong> is looser, spectrum language — it emphasizes minimizing animal products
        and maximizing whole plant foods (fruits, vegetables, legumes, whole grains, nuts, seeds) without
        requiring full exclusion, so someone "eating plant-based" may still occasionally eat meat, dairy, or
        eggs. Vegan is a strict subset of plant-based, not a synonym for it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">The Research</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Associated with lower BMI, lower rates of type 2 diabetes and heart disease, and lower all-cause
        mortality in large cohort studies — with the important caveat that the benefit is concentrated in <em>healthful</em> plant-based patterns (whole grains, produce, legumes, nuts) rather than "unhealthful"
        ones built on refined carbs and sugar.
      </p>
      <strong>Cohort study:</strong> Orlich MJ, Singh PN, Sabaté J, et al. "Vegetarian Dietary Patterns and
      Mortality in Adventist Health Study 2," <em>JAMA Internal Medicine</em>, 2013. Following 73,308
      Seventh-day Adventists, vegetarian diets — including a vegan subgroup — were associated with lower
      all-cause and cardiovascular mortality than non-vegetarian diets.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23836264/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the cohort study →</a>
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Additional research:</strong>
      <br /><br />
      Qian F, Liu G, Hu FB, Bhupathiraju SN, Sun Q. "Association Between Plant-Based Dietary Patterns and Risk
      of Type 2 Diabetes: A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis," <em>JAMA Internal Medicine</em>, 2019. Greater
      adherence to plant-based patterns — especially "healthful" ones — was associated with significantly
      lower type 2 diabetes risk; "unhealthful" plant-based patterns were not protective.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31329220/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the meta-analysis →</a>
      <br /><br />
      Quek J, Lim G, Lim WH, et al. "The Association of Plant-Based Diet With Cardiovascular Disease and
      Mortality: A Meta-Analysis and Systematic Review of Prospective Cohort Studies," <em>Frontiers in
      Cardiovascular Medicine</em>, 2021. Across 13 cohorts (~410,000 people), higher plant-based adherence
      was linked to roughly 8–10% lower cardiovascular disease incidence and mortality — again, concentrated
      in healthful patterns.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8604150/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the meta-analysis →</a>
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Nutrient-adequacy caveat:</strong> Melina V, Craig W, Levin S. "Position of the Academy of
      Nutrition and Dietetics: Vegetarian Diets," <em>Journal of the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics</em>, 2016. States that well-planned vegetarian and vegan diets are nutritionally adequate across life stages
      — but explicitly flags vitamin B12 (absent from plant foods entirely), iron and zinc (lower bioavailability
      from non-heme sources), long-chain omega-3s, vitamin D, calcium, and iodine as nutrients that need
      deliberate attention, particularly on a fully vegan diet.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27886704/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the position paper →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Food Examples</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        <strong>Protein:</strong> lentils, chickpeas, black beans, tofu, tempeh, seitan, edamame.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Whole grains:</strong> quinoa, brown rice, oats, farro.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Nuts & seeds:</strong> almonds, walnuts, chia, flax, hemp seeds.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>B12 sources for strict vegans:</strong> fortified nutritional yeast, fortified plant milks and
        cereals, or a B12 supplement — per the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics, this is the one nutrient
        that has no reliable unfortified plant source, so it isn't optional for a fully vegan diet.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">A Sample Day</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        <strong>Breakfast:</strong> oats with fortified soy milk, chia seeds, and berries.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Lunch:</strong> lentil soup with whole-grain bread and a tahini-dressed salad.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Snack:</strong> hummus with vegetables, or a handful of almonds.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Dinner:</strong> tofu-and-vegetable stir-fry over brown rice with edamame.
        <br /><br />
        Include a B12-fortified food or supplement daily if eating strictly vegan.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Who It Might Suit</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Tends to suit people motivated by environmental or ethical concerns, or by the cardiometabolic risk
        reduction the research above points to, and who are willing to plan meals rather than eat on
        autopilot. The Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics' position is that these diets are appropriate for
        athletes and all life stages "when appropriately planned" — that qualifier matters. Without deliberate
        attention to B12, iron, omega-3s, vitamin D, and calcium, or for people with very high calorie and
        protein needs, real gaps can occur, especially on a fully vegan diet with less margin for error than a
        looser plant-based pattern that still includes some animal foods.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

